WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.220
INTRO:
We all know Robert Herjavec as the chill guy

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:04.669
sitting in the right corner in Shark Tank.

00:00:04.669 --> 00:00:08.900
In many episodes, we hear Robert talk about
his background in software and his rough upbringing

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:14.570
as an immigrant.

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:23.470
But, it’s never made clear how he made his
fortune and what exactly he’s doing now.

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:27.160
Aside from a vague background, superfans of
the show are often quick to argue that Robert’s

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:32.360
chill demeanor is just a facade, and that
in reality, Robert is a rather passive aggressive

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:35.140
dude that never follows through with his deals.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:40.270
According to Forbes, within the first 7 seasons
of Shark Tank, Robert made 24 deals on air,

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:42.860
but only 11 of these actually closed.

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:47.980
This means that Robert’s closing rate was
only 45.8% which is the lowest of all the

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:50.610
sharks and nearly half of Mark’s closing
rate.

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:56.280
So, who really is Robert Herjavec and how
did he get rich in the first place?

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:02.640
ROUGH UPBRINGING:
Taking a look back, Robert’s backstory isn’t

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:04.840
just some sob story for TV.

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:09.330
He really did have a pretty difficult childhood
growing up as he was born in Varazdin, Croatia

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:12.230
on September 14, 1962.

00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:15.770
At the time, this territory was controlled
by Yugoslavia.

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:20.520
Yugoslavia wasn’t as strict as some other
red countries, but they were still communist

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:22.060
and they upheld its principles.

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:26.900
Robert’s family was never big fans of these
ideologies, and Robert’s father was actually

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:29.570
repeatedly jailed for talking out against
the regime.

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:34.950
Apparently, it was common for him to get drunk
and go on rants about how bad the system was.

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:38.780
Despite their differing views though, the
family put up with Yugoslavia up until Robert

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:42.650
was 8 years old when the family immigrated
to Halifax, Canada.

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:47.170
When they arrived in Canada, the family only
had a suitcase filled with clothes and $20,

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:50.921
so Robert’s father instantly started looking
for a job and eventually found a job at a

00:01:50.921 --> 00:01:53.080
factory earning $76 per week.

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:57.640
It’s not clear if he was actually a janitor
at the factory, but Robert has mentioned many

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:00.980
times that his father used to mop floors at
a factory.

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:05.010
As for Robert, despite these extremely hard
circumstances growing up, he says that he

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:07.210
was never mad about his situation.

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:11.030
He didn’t even know how life was on the
other side of the world, so he had nothing

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:12.440
to compare to.

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:16.740
Once Robert started attending school in Canada
though, he started to complain a bit.

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:20.510
You see, Robert didn’t know any English
and many of his classmates would bully him

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:21.510
about it.

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:25.720
One day, he came home and complained to his
mom about how mean all of his classmates were.

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:29.820
Though his mother comforted him, his dad told
him to stop complaining and have some pride

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:31.410
and courage in himself.

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:35.200
Ever since this experience, Robert says he
got very good at just putting his head down

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:39.500
and working through hardships, and unfortunately
for Robert, there were many more hardships

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:40.500
to come.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:44.200
Robert would end up attending New College
at the University of Toronto and graduate

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:47.050
with a degree in English literature and political
science.

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:51.260
In the meantime, Robert took on many side
jobs working minimum wage to help his family

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:52.280
financially.

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:55.840
He took on roles as a waiter, a newspaper
delivery man, and a retail salesman.

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:59.819
Things didn’t get much better after college
either as Robert would struggle to find a

00:02:59.819 --> 00:03:03.120
job and he would end up stumbling into the
film field.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:07.460
He didn’t become an A list director or anything,
but he was able to score some nice short term

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:08.460
gigs.

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:11.780
For example, he served as a third assistant
director for the movies Cain and Abel and

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:13.540
The Return of Billy Jack.

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:17.660
He was also a field producer for Global TV
for the 1984 winter olympics.

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:21.460
While Robert enjoyed these opportunities,
none of these provided him with a stable income

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:23.970
and he was still waiting tables to make ends
meet.

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:28.150
What Robert really wanted was a job in the
fast growing tech industry, but no one would

00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:31.570
consider him due his lack of background and
experience.

00:03:31.570 --> 00:03:35.350
Instead of complaining though, Robert looked
for ways that he could gain this knowledge,

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:38.459
and the easiest idea that came to mind was
offering to work for free.

00:03:38.459 --> 00:03:43.150
A company called Logiquest would eventually
agree to this offer, and after working for

00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:47.140
six months for free at Logiquest while still
waiting tables, Robert would secure a full

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:52.900
time position and finally break into the tech
industry.

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:56.120
FIRED:
Despite working so hard to get the job though,

00:03:56.120 --> 00:04:00.840
Robert was only able to stick around for a
few years as he was let go in 1990.

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:04.840
By this point, Robert was fed up with trying
to prove himself to employers, and decided

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:06.459
to start his own business.

00:04:06.459 --> 00:04:09.910
He says that he never had any large ambitions
of being a business owner or anything like

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:10.910
that.

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:13.480
At the time, starting a business seemed like
the only way to continue progressing in his

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:16.039
career and that’s why he took the plunge.

00:04:16.039 --> 00:04:20.320
To this day, Robert is a massive proponent
of working a 9 to 5, and thinks that he’d

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:23.720
probably still be working a 9 to 5 if he wasn’t
fired from Logiquest.

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:28.230
Anyway, Robert started his first company with
Warren Avis who is the founder of Avis Rent

00:04:28.230 --> 00:04:29.230
a Car.

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:33.460
It’s not clear what the business was called
or what they even did, but the most important

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:35.380
part of this business was the experience with
Warren.

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:41.370
Warren became a sort of unofficial mentor
to Robert, and was pivotal in his future businesses.

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:44.940
One experience with Warren that was especially
influential was a story about hot dogs.

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:46.380
It looks like the duo's business had something
to do with sales like all businesses, and

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:50.000
one day, Warren told Robert that his approach
to sales was terrible.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:53.530
Warren compared Robert’s sales style to
that of a hot dog vendor.

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:58.000
Robert was desperately peddling as much product
as possible on a daily basis to simply make

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:00.150
ends meet like the hot dog vendor.

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:04.090
But at the end of the day, it wasn’t the
hot dog vendor that made the big money; the

00:05:04.090 --> 00:05:07.430
person who made the big money was the hot
dog vendor's supplier.

00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:12.250
Warren advised Robert to aim to be the supplier
not the vendor, and this story really resonated

00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:13.250
with Robert.

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:16.990
But this wasn’t very realistic for him at
the time as he was just trying to make enough

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:19.530
money to afford his monthly mortgage payments.

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:23.500
Once his business with Warren grew to a moderate
size though, Robert would sell his stake for

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:27.660
$60,000, and focused on starting a business
where he could be the supplier, and that brings

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:29.390
us into BRAK systems.

00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:35.020
HITTING IT BIG:
BRAK systems was focused on developing internet

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:36.850
security software similar to McAfee.

00:05:36.850 --> 00:05:40.830
And, given that the internet was just in its
infancy at that point, Robert was able to

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:44.770
ride the wave up through the 1990s, and BRAK
systems would become the largest internet

00:05:44.770 --> 00:05:47.770
security provider in Canada by the end of
the decade.

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:51.511
Before he knew it, he was getting acquisition
offers left and right, and it didn’t take

00:05:51.511 --> 00:05:55.840
him long to secure a deal with AT&amp;T Canada
for $30.2 million in 2000.

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:59.389
The deal was almost finalized, but it was
up to Robert to choose if we wanted to cash

00:05:59.389 --> 00:06:01.370
out in stock or cash.

00:06:01.370 --> 00:06:04.720
All of his lawyers and business buddies were
telling him that taking the stock was a no

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:05.720
brainer.

00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:08.710
He could defer taxes and make much more money
long term.

00:06:08.710 --> 00:06:12.510
But Robert’s father told him to take the
cash, and Robert would end up listening to

00:06:12.510 --> 00:06:13.510
his father.

00:06:13.510 --> 00:06:17.400
For nearly two years after the acquisition,
Robert says that he was very triggered as

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:21.060
AT&amp;T stock just kept going up as the dotcom
bubble roared on.

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:26.370
But, as we now know, the bubble would burst
and AT&amp;T Canada would end up filing for bankruptcy

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:27.370
protection.

00:06:27.370 --> 00:06:31.740
Now, AT&amp;T Canada did end up surviving, but
I think we’d agree that taking the cash

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:34.940
buyout was a way better path than going through
a bankruptcy.

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:38.449
Objectively though, Robert wouldn’t have
really been hurt financially even if he took

00:06:38.449 --> 00:06:41.990
the stock as he also had another business
that was booming.

00:06:41.990 --> 00:06:45.990
Right after he sold BRAK, Robert joined a
startup called RAMP networks as vice president.

00:06:45.990 --> 00:06:50.650
RAMP networks also provided internet security
software, but unlike BRAK systems, RAMP also

00:06:50.650 --> 00:06:53.400
provided broadband access solutions like routers.

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:57.070
Robert didn’t work at RAMP for too long
though as RAMP would be bought by Nokia in

00:06:57.070 --> 00:07:00.380
January of 2001 for $127 million.

00:07:00.380 --> 00:07:04.449
I doubt Robert owned very much of the company
given how long he worked at the company, but

00:07:04.449 --> 00:07:08.240
considering the magnitude of the acquisition,
even just a few percent works out to millions

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:09.240
of dollars.

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:13.509
So, Robert was easily a multi millionaire
if not a deca millionaire, and Robert would

00:07:13.509 --> 00:07:15.490
decide that it was time to retire.

00:07:15.490 --> 00:07:19.800
His wife was still working, so Robert became
a stay at home dad and focused on raising

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:20.860
their children.

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:23.889
Robert says that this felt wonderful at first.

00:07:23.889 --> 00:07:27.800
He didn’t have to worry about money, he
could do whatever he wanted, and he was building

00:07:27.800 --> 00:07:29.330
experiences with his children.

00:07:29.330 --> 00:07:31.570
It seemed like things couldn’t get any better.

00:07:31.570 --> 00:07:35.460
But, as his children started going to school
and he was the only one at home, it didn’t

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:39.320
take him long to start wondering WTF am I
doing with my life.

00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:43.540
After just 3 years of retirement, Robert decided
that the retirement life simply wasn’t for

00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:47.639
him and that he far preferred building businesses
especially given that he could now build them

00:07:47.639 --> 00:07:50.100
out of passion as opposed to necessity.

00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:54.960
And with that, Robert founded the Herjavec
group in 2003.

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:58.389
CHILLING:
Robert didn’t bet the bank with the Herjavec

00:07:58.389 --> 00:07:59.520
group like Elon Musk though.

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:02.420
Instead, he preferred to start up the company
with just $20,000.

00:08:02.420 --> 00:08:06.870
Clearly, he didn’t want to be broke again
if the business failed which is smart.

00:08:06.870 --> 00:08:11.150
But really, Robert didn’t have much to worry
about as the Herjavec group was another internet

00:08:11.150 --> 00:08:15.050
security company, and he was quite experienced
in the field by this point.

00:08:15.050 --> 00:08:18.990
The Herjavec group initially specialized in
email anti spam detection, but they have since

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:23.370
expanded into all sorts of cybersecurity services,
and their revenue has closely followed.

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:27.490
In their first year of business, the Herjavec
Group pulled in $400,000 in sales which is

00:08:27.490 --> 00:08:33.059
already quite nice, but by 2012, this number
would explode to $125 million.

00:08:33.059 --> 00:08:37.990
Today, the Herjavec group is the largest internet
security firm in Canada and pulls in $223

00:08:37.990 --> 00:08:40.189
million per year in revenue.

00:08:40.189 --> 00:08:43.869
Unlike his last businesses though, Robert
has no intention of selling the Herjavec Group.

00:08:43.869 --> 00:08:47.860
He’s looking to grow the company into a
multi billion company, and at this point,

00:08:47.860 --> 00:08:50.560
it’s just a matter of time until he gets
there.

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:55.149
According to Google, Robert is currently worth
$200 million, but I’m pretty sure that this

00:08:55.149 --> 00:08:56.420
number is out of date.

00:08:56.420 --> 00:09:01.170
I’ve seen Google display this number for
at least 5 years, and I’m pretty sure Robert

00:09:01.170 --> 00:09:04.930
hasn’t been stagnant for 5 years especially
in today’s market environment.

00:09:04.930 --> 00:09:09.740
So, I think a more realistic estimate is probably
$300 to $500 million if not more.

00:09:09.740 --> 00:09:13.810
So, Robert is well on his way to becoming
a billionaire, but he’s not in any rush

00:09:13.810 --> 00:09:15.189
to get there either.

00:09:15.189 --> 00:09:18.779
Robert has been enjoying the process, and
he’s been spending a lot of time on hobbies

00:09:18.779 --> 00:09:20.430
like racing and Shark Tank.

00:09:20.430 --> 00:09:24.809
In 2011 for instance, he won the rookie of
the year award at the Ferrari Challenge.

00:09:24.809 --> 00:09:29.480
As for TV, Robert made his TV debut in 2003
on Dragon’s Den and he has since become

00:09:29.480 --> 00:09:31.970
a regular investor on Shark Tank as well.

00:09:31.970 --> 00:09:34.529
And that brings us into all the Shark Tank
controversy.

00:09:34.529 --> 00:09:39.399
SHARK TANK CONTROVERSY:
One of the biggest critiques against Robert

00:09:39.399 --> 00:09:43.550
is that he’s often passive aggressive towards
some of the other sharks and especially Mark.

00:09:43.550 --> 00:09:47.889
There’s really no arguing against this as
it’s oftentimes very clear, and I think

00:09:47.889 --> 00:09:51.350
this really stems from Robert’s insecurity
of not being wanted.

00:09:51.350 --> 00:09:55.220
Most entrepreneurs who come onto Shark Tank
want to make a deal with Mark or Lori.

00:09:55.220 --> 00:09:59.459
Sometimes they want Daymond for clothing businesses,
but basically no one comes in looking to make

00:09:59.459 --> 00:10:04.019
a deal with Kevin, Barbara, or Robert which
is a shame given that Kevin and Robert are

00:10:04.019 --> 00:10:05.459
awesome sharks.

00:10:05.459 --> 00:10:09.490
Kevin chooses to play into this persona by
ironically calling himself Mr. Wonderful and

00:10:09.490 --> 00:10:10.779
squeezing the other sharks.

00:10:10.779 --> 00:10:14.739
Meanwhile, Barbara acts like nothing is going
on and that entrepreneurs love her.

00:10:14.739 --> 00:10:19.040
I think Robert, however, is a bit triggered
by this and that this is what leads to his

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:21.139
occasional passive aggressiveness.

00:10:21.139 --> 00:10:24.399
And as for his close rate, while it's true
that he has the lowest close rate on Shark

00:10:24.399 --> 00:10:28.990
Tank, it should be noted that the sample size
is rather small and the other sharks except

00:10:28.990 --> 00:10:30.339
Mark aren’t much better.

00:10:30.339 --> 00:10:32.649
They all hover at about 50/50.

00:10:32.649 --> 00:10:36.639
Also, something else to keep in mind is that
the low number may be the entrepreneurs fault

00:10:36.639 --> 00:10:37.639
as well.

00:10:37.639 --> 00:10:42.389
We know that most entrepreneurs gravitate
towards Mark and Lori, so maybe the problem

00:10:42.389 --> 00:10:45.029
is that Robert just ends up with lower quality
entrepreneurs.

00:10:45.029 --> 00:10:49.769
As for Mark’s exceptionally high closing
rate, I suspect that this is just an ego play.

00:10:49.769 --> 00:10:54.680
I bet that he likely feels 50/50 about many
of his deals as well, but he ends up following

00:10:54.680 --> 00:10:57.230
through anyway just because he can.

00:10:57.230 --> 00:11:00.820
Mark cares more about keeping his word and
following through than losing half a million

00:11:00.820 --> 00:11:04.069
dollars, and that’s completely fine given
that he’s worth 4 times more than the rest

00:11:04.069 --> 00:11:05.420
of the regular sharks combined.

00:11:05.420 --> 00:11:09.199
Also, it’s not like Robert hasn’t invested
a large amount himself.

00:11:09.199 --> 00:11:14.269
As of 2019, Robert committed to investing
a total of $16 million on Shark Tank, and

00:11:14.269 --> 00:11:18.749
even if only half of those deals closed, that’s
nearly $10 million which is probably a significant

00:11:18.749 --> 00:11:21.420
amount if not a majority of Robert’s liquid
wealth.

00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:24.740
And, at the end of the day, I think Robert
says it best.

00:11:24.740 --> 00:11:27.029
Shark Tank is not a business show that’s
on TV.

00:11:27.029 --> 00:11:32.319
It’s a TV show that happens to be about
business, but that’s just what I think.

00:11:32.319 --> 00:11:34.869
Are some of you still convinced Robert is
a douchebag?

00:11:34.869 --> 00:11:36.139
Comment that down below.

00:11:36.139 --> 00:11:39.850
Also, drop a like if you respect Robert.

00:11:39.850 --> 00:11:43.399
And of course, consider joining our discord
community to suggest future video ideas and

00:11:43.399 --> 00:11:49.130
consider subscribing to see more questions
logically answered.

